#Type Pattern
-in Java 16 instanceof was extended to take a type pattern and perform matching.
this simplified the instaceof-cast idiom,resulting in more concise and less error-pron
code
#before java 16
if(obj instanceof String){
String s=(String)obj
}
#After java 16
if(obj instanceof String S){
  sop(s.toUpperCase())
}

as their is no casting with type patterns, this style is more declarative



#Records

1-Records are special type of class that save lot of boilerplate code. they are considered "data carriers" adn are immutable
2- Records are specified using a record declaration where you specify the "components" of the record.
3-These components become "Final" instace variable and accessor methods having the same names as the components are provided automatically
4- In addition, a constructor,toString(),equals() and hashCode() methods are also generated
public record Dog(String name,Integer age){}
-Code that receives an instance of record class typically
extracts the data(components),using the build in component accessors Methods
-A "Record Pattern" consists of a type ,a component pattern list(which may be empty) and an optional identifier

#A record pattern does 2 things for us

1-checks if an object passes the instanceof test
2-disaggregates the record instance into its component

Record pattern support nesting

public record Person(String name,Integer age)
//Before java 21
if( obj instanceof Person p){
  String name=p.name();
  int age=p.age();
  sop(name+""+age)
}
#After java 21
//Person(Strings,Integer age) is a "record pattern" which does 2 things
1-Test wheather the objects is of type Person(as usual)
2-Extracts the records components by invoking the accessor Methods on our behalf

if(obj instanceof Person(String s,Integer age){
   System.out.println(s+" "+age)
}